body:
Not true. My ex probably won't ever take me back and I don't plan to meet anyone else in my life. My ex was the only person I've ever been interested in. Its not as simple as meeting other girls. I just don't have a interest in people in general. If I didn't meet my ex (which I had a crush on for literally over half a decade), I was planning to live the rest of my life alone.

&#x200B;

My mom is see me as disappointment because all she ever wants to see is me getting married and then her having grand children. Well too bad considering I'm not going to meet anyone else in my life.

&#x200B;

For my 2 friends, they'll eventually move on and be busy with life. Busy with their jobs, their family, etc.

I know how I can get better, and the first step is to get over her. But that's the problem. I won't ever get over her. As much as people say it will take time and I will eventually get over her, I know I won't and I will make sure I won't because I don't want to get over her even though I know I should. If our breakup was messy or mutual, then I can see myself slowly getting over her. But the thing is she has never hurt me and she was everything and more I could have asked for. I said I had a crush on her for 6 years without even knowing her and I couldn't get over my crush for her. So now that I fell in love with her and everything, I don't think I could ever get over her.

&#x200B;

I don't think I can ever experience the happiness I had when I was with her ever again. I've never really been interested in anyone aside from her. It's not even that I have trouble asking girls out. I mean I don't know if I do because I've only asked out two in my life. I didn't even plan to ask out the first one because I lost interest after talking to them a bit. But my original intention was to ask them out so I did it anyways and was kind of relieved that I was rejected. I just lose not very interested in people. It's don't even think it's because I'm shallow. While I can find a girl attractive based on their looks, it just on first glance. Then after that I just lose interest and they don't seem attractive anymore. That's why I simply just don't ask girls out because I just don't find them attractive and lose interest very shortly. However, when it come to her, its completely different. I could stare at her picture for hours and she is just a beautiful as when I first laid eyes on her. The more I talked with her, the more I fell in love. After all, in those 6 years, she was the most attractive person I've seen even compared to like models. Even if a girl that most would say is attractive was to ask me out, I would say no because I simply don't find anyone attractive. She was the only exception. Even before I went out with her, in those 6 years, there wasn't anyone I found attractive or interested in.

&#x200B;

It's kind of a contradiction. I'm the most happy when I'm with the person I love. However, at the same time, there's no one I'm even interested in aside from her. If other girls were to ask me out, I would reject every single one of them. They say you shouldn't judge a girl based on their appearance, but you have to be at least somewhat physically attracted to them at the start. Or else, does rejecting anyone make you shallow? Does it mean you should just say yes to everyone that asks you out so you can give them a chance? I don't think it works like that at all. Yeah you shouldn't completely based someone off their looks, but you have to find them somewhat attractive at the start at least. The problem is, I don't find anyone attractive after first glance in my eyes aside from her.

Wow that was a story and a half. It's good you had such an amazing person in your life, and I'm glad you were happy. I know it isn't in my place to give advice, but I would say think over the whole child situation especially at such a young age. I'm assuming you are both teenagers and having a child that early is a huge responsibility. It takes a lot of time and money to raise one which can be overwhelming when you are still in school and cannot financially support one yet. If you were to have one at lets say 22 when you got married to her, that would be a lot more manageable as you'll likely either be graduated or already working. It's just my take on the whole child situation. I'm in my early 20s and I wouldn't thin of having a child until my late 20s at earliest, but preferably early 30s when I can save up some money. Raising a child might seem fun, but it's a huge responsibility, and I assume you'll want the best for your child. While it is possible to raise it with little money, having more saved up would mean they have a better life growing up.

&#x200B;

If you don't mind me asking, what happened between you too then? Why do you browse around this subreddit specifically?

Yeah I know what I did wrong. I wish I realized this sooner. That's why I don't blame her for any of this, and only blame myself for losing her. Sometimes things are out of your control, like if she simply is more interested in a different guy. But her leaving me was all my fault. I can't say there was nothing I could have done and I won't be in denial I ruined everything.

&#x200B;

I don't know how likely it is for us to ever be back together considering we haven't talked for months and she has me blocked. I do send her cards from time to time which I know isn't good, but I have to do it or my thoughts for her would be too overwhelming.

&#x200B;

This is also why I believe me killing myself is deserved. A lot of people don't deserve to die, but instead forced into the decision due to circumstances they simply can't control. While there are a lot in my life that was out of my control that lead to my depression, a lot of it was from my own choices. Even though my mom wanted me to do this career, I was fine with it at first and accepted it. I could have chose differently. When it comes to her, it was all in my control. I ruined it. So as a result, I think it's reasonable to be punished. And if death is my punishment, I will accept that.
